l --require-hashes` for `policy-check.yml` + `release-gate.yml` pyyaml/jsonschema installs.~~ ✅ **closed in slice 020M** (this PR's branch). `requirements/policy-check.in` (top-level pins) + `requirements/policy-check.txt` (generated by `pip-compile --generate-hashes`, full transitive closure with all-platform wheel SHA256s) added. All 5 install sites (4× `policy-check.yml` + 1× `release-gate.yml`) switched to `pip install --require-hashes -r requirements/policy-check.txt`. Top-level pins unchanged from v1.0.0 (`pyyaml==6.0.2` + `jsonschema==4.23.0`); only the install mechanism is hardened. PATCH bump v1.0.0 → v1.0.1 per VERSIONING.md \"internal refactors\". D-038 filed.\n- ~~**TF-020H3rg-003**: Auto-open `release-gate-violation` issue on push:tags failure~~ ✅ **closed in slice 020H.3.1** (this PR's branch). `.github/workflows/release-gate.yml` split into two jobs: `release-gate` (evaluation; `contents: read`) + `release-gate-violation-issue` (`needs: release-gate`; `if: needs.release-gate.result == 'failure' && github.event_name == 'push'`; `permissions: { contents: read, issues: write }`; pre-creates labels via `gh label create --force`; de-dups by tag substring; uses Python templating in both new-issue and comment branches). Ratified by **D-037** (issues:write job-level scope). Forward-compat assignee env: `SCP_RELEASE_GATE_ASSIGNEES` (defaults to `jrnb2024`).\n- **TF-020H3rg-004**: Issue auto-close on corrective tag-cut — when the operator cuts the corrected `v<X>.<Y+1>.0`, the open `release-gate-violation` issue isn't auto-closed. Operator closes manually after the triage checklist completes. Closure path: a future workflow step that detects the corrected tag-cut and `gh issue close`s any open release-gate-violation issue for the bad tag-series. No deadline (forward-compat). Filed at 020H.3.1 R1 completeness COMP-MIN-001 closure.\n\n## Tracked-forward items from 020M (supply-chain hash-pinning)\n\n- ~~**TF-020M-001**: `conflict-gate.yml` retains `pip install pyyaml>=6.0` (line ~42) without hash-pinning.~~ ✅ **closed in slice 020N** (this PR's branch). `requirements/conflict-gate.in` (top-level pins matching `pyproject.toml` `[project.dependencies]` + `[project.optional-dependencies.dev]`) + `requirements/conflict-gate.txt` (generated by `pip-compile --generate-hashes` under Python 3.12, full transitive closure with all-platform wheel SHA256s) added. `.github/workflows/conflict-gate.yml` \"Install Python dependencies\" step restructured to: (i) hash-pinned `pip install --require-hashes -r requirements/conflict-gate.txt`; (ii) local vendored `ct-auth` wheel installed `--no-deps`; (iii) editable local SCP package installed `--no-deps`; (iv) post-install version-pin assertion (mirrors 020M SAFE-MAJ-002 pattern). No semver bump (conflict-gate is internal-only per VERSIONING.md §\"Scope\"). D-039 filed.\n- ~~**TF-020M-002**: ADOPT-001 §12.7.13 lockfile regeneration procedure does not warn that the hardcoded version-pin assertion strings must be updated in lockstep when bumping the lockfile.~~ ✅ **already inline-closed in slice 020M fix-round-2** (commit `37bbf5e`). The REGENERATION INVARIANT callout added to ADOPT-001 §12.7.13 explicitly names the four hardcoded `assert yaml.__version__ ==` / `assert jsonschema.__version__ ==` sites that must be updated in lockstep. The TF entry filed by the R2 safety reviewer was redundant with the inline closure; pruned in slice 020N STATUS.md update.\n\n## Tracked-forward items from 020N (conflict-gate hash-pinning)\n\n- **TF-020N-001**: `conflict-gate.yml` `permissions: id-token: write` is workflow-default scoped (line ~23) and applies across all steps including the new pip-install step (slice 020N). The `id-token: write` was originally added for `gh attestation verify` of OPA/Conftest binaries (a pre-020N step). With slice 020N's hash-pinned pip install above the attestation step, the `id-token: write` exposure surface during the pip-install network call is now broader than necessary — pip itself does not need OIDC. Closure path: narrow the privilege to job-level or step-level scoping so the new pip-install step runs with `contents: read` only. Forward-compat; symmetric posture with D-037's `release-gate-violation-issue` job-level scoping (slice 020H.3.1). Filed at 020N R1 safety SAFE-MIN-003 closure.\n\n## Tracked-forward items from 020L (first rule-RFC dogfood)\n\n- **TF-020L-001**: Unicode-whitespace regex divergence between Python `re` (Unicode-aware `\\S`) and OPA RE2 (ASCII-only `\\s`). For the §6.2 ASCII fixture corpus the divergence is irrelevant; a future fixture with Unicode whitespace in a URL-like string may surface conflict-gate flap. Closure path: Phase-2 implementation monitors SCP self-dogfood gate for `SCP-E005` flap on Unicode-whitespace input; if observed, anchor the regex pattern to ASCII-only printable range (e.g. `https?://[\\x21-\\x7e]+`) in both engines and add a fixture. Close as no-op if no divergence surfaces during the warn-baseline observation window. **Status at v1.1.0 cut (2026-05-02): no flap observed across slice 020P CI, release-gate dry-run, and post-tag observer; continue monitoring across estate adopters until ~30-90d post-v1.1.0.** Forward-compat; no deadline. Filed at 020L R1 correctness COR-NIT-001 closure.\n- ~~**TF-020L-002**: RULE-TEMPLATE.md §5 lacks residual-bypass guidance for rules enforcing syntactic-only patterns.~~ ✅ **closed in slice 020P** (PR #91, commit `d83ce52`); RULE-TEMPLATE.md §5 amended with \"Residual known bypass (for syntactic-pattern rules)\" guidance pointing at RULE-001 §5 case 5 as worked example.\n- ~~**TF-020L-003**: README.md §3 Merge says \"An entry in `docs/DECISIONS.md` if the rule introduces a new domain...\" but does NOT define \"new domain\".~~ ✅ **closed in slice 020P** (PR #91, commit `d83ce52`); README.md §3 amended with \"new domain\" definition (new top-level input schema OR no existing SCP-R-NNN counterpart; same-input-schema additions are same-domain).\n\n## Tracked-forward items from 020P (RULE-001 Phase 2)\n\n---FILE6---\n# Standards Control Plane — Decision Log\n\n**Last Updated:** 2026-05-09 (D-044 filed for WP-SCP-024 024B-core; estate cascade scope split)\n\n> **D-021 reservation note (2026-04-28):** D-021 is reserved for the\n> 2026-05-31 atomic workday filing per WP-SCP-019 hygiene response (PR\n> #30, 2026-04-21). Codex executors running WP-SCP-022 implementation\n> slices must NOT assign D-021 to any decision filed during the\n> autonomous run. The WP-SCP-022 §4.7 gate-enforcement helper\n> (`scripts/wp_scp_022_gate_check.sh --check-d021`) detects collisions.\n\n> **D-041 / D-042 / D-043 reservation note (2026-05-03, plan-doc 023A):**\n> D-041 is reserved for WP-SCP-023 slice 023B (cross-repo scorecard data\n> shape + opt-in adopter participation model). D-042 is reserved for\n> slice 023C (aggregator pipeline trust model — GitHub Actions Artifact\n> Attestation for adopter emits + SCP-side Ed25519 key registry).\n> **Mandatory trust constraint on D-042 (closes 023A R2 MAJ-SAFE-R2-001,\n> per WP-SCP-023 §5 step 3):** the 023C aggregator MUST verify the\n> `job_workflow_ref` OIDC claim in each artifact attestation against the\n> SCP reusable workflow path AND SHA pin via `gh attestation verify\n> --signer-workflow <owner>/<repo>/.github/workflows/policy-check.yml@<sha>`;\n> OIDC JWKS signature verification alone is not sufficient.\n> D-043 is reserved for slice 023D (MCP method `scp.consult_scorecard`\n> shape + read-only contract). Codex executors dispatched for any\n> WP-SCP-023 implementation slice must NOT assign D-041, D-042, or D-043\n> to any other decision filed during that slice. The reservation pattern\n> mirrors the D-021 guard above.\n\n> **D-044 / D-045 / D-046 reservation note (2026-05-03, plan-doc 024A):**\n> D-044 was reserved for WP-SCP-024 slice 024B (`scripts/scaffold-downstream.sh`\n> = `SCP-073-scaffolder` operational contract + adopter-template versioning\n> + invocation-log convention extension). It is now filed as ACCEPTED in\n> 024B-core; the deferred 024B-extras surface carries the `--restore`\n> rollback mode and workflow wiring. D-045 is reserved for slice 024C (estate\n> cascade ordering canary-first per WP-SCP-024 §5.1, per-adopter\n> onboarding contract per §5.2, post-bake observation window ≥1 calendar\n> week + ≥1 Renovate bump cycle per invariant 8, cascade-slice rollback\n> procedure per invariant 7). D-046 is reserved for slice 024G\n> (estate-cascade Threshold A criteria + post-Threshold-A maintenance\n> posture: recurring drift remediation, version-ramp coordination,\n> departing-adopter procedure; decision on whether `scp.consult_estate_status`\n> ships standalone or rolls into existing `consult_scorecard`). Codex\n> executors dispatched for any WP-SCP-024 implementation slice must NOT\n> assign D-044, D-045, or D-046 to any other decision filed during that\n> slice. The reservation pattern mirrors the D-021 / D-041–043 guards\n> above.\n\n| ID | Date | Decision | Status | Rationale |\n|----|------|----------|--------|-----------|\n| D-001 | 2026-04-11 | Use Python for the phase 1 CLI-first implementation | ACCEPTED | Fast path to explicit schemas, local tooling, JSON validation, and deterministic file-based execution |\n| D-002 | 2026-04-11 | Keep the project as a standalone app rather than extending docs-agent or Control Tower directly | ACCEPTED | Keeps evaluator runtime independent while still allowing later integration with both systems |\n| D-003 | 2026-04-11 | Limit the first governed implementation slice to standards registry loading, minimal findings loading, and consult retrieval | ACCEPTED | Keeps the first end-to-end work package narrow enough to review rigorously while still allowing real consult output |\n| D-004 | 2026-04-11 | Introduce an explicit project-area intermediate representation before evaluator implementation | ACCEPTED | Keeps evaluator inputs deterministic, testable, and independent from raw repo sprawl |\n| D-005 | 2026-04-11 | Wire governance evaluation through the audit CLI before attempting architecture evaluation | ACCEPTED | Establishes one real evaluator path end to end before widening the audit surface |\n| D-006 | 2026-04-11 | Extend audit and project-area contracts before WP-SCP-003 implementation | ACCEPTED | Governance review evidence and unsupported-domain coverage must be first-class structured data, not implied by prose or raw path scanning |\n| D-007 | 2026-04-11 | Use an explicit unattended-delivery protocol for the remaining programme | ACCEPTED | The remaining work should continue without waiting for user answers, using documented default decisions, bounded review loops, and backlog carry-forward for non-blocking residuals |\n| D-008 | 2026-04-12 | Use explicit file-content signal heuristics for phase-1 architecture evaluation | ACCEPTED | The architecture slice needs deterministic, test-backed checks against repo-local evidence rather than broad semantic inference or prompt-only judgement |\n| D-009 | 2026-04-12 | Add a second repo-backed fixture corpus for architecture signal coverage | ACCEPTED | `ARCH-003` and `ARCH-004` need concrete repo-backed sample files instead of synthetic payload-only tests if the slice is going to prove its own honesty |\n| D-010 | 2026-04-12 | Reconcile findings by deterministic finding ID and area-scoped audit coverage | ACCEPTED | Findings persistence must refresh and resolve records only inside the audited area and evaluated domains so unrelated history is not disturbed |\n| D-011 | 2026-04-12 | Guard findings persistence with scope-collision checks and per-file atomic replacement plus rollback | ACCEPTED | Persisted lifecycle state must fail on `finding_id` scope collisions and should avoid half-written files without pretending phase 1 has a crash-safe multi-file transaction |\n| D-012 | 2026-04-12 | Bind generated report and area-summary timestamps to the current audit timestamp | ACCEPTED | Report outputs need byte-stable reruns and freshness checks, so they should reuse `audit_result.generated_at` rather than inventing wall-clock timestamps |\n| D-013 | 2026-04-12 | Reuse the current write-flow guarantees for report artifacts and carry true bundled transactions to backlog | ACCEPTED | The reporting slice should stay downstream of the existing persistence guarantees instead of pretending to offer a crash-safe cross-artifact commit in phase 1 |\n| D-014 | 2026-04-12 | Guard `report` freshness with embedded findings-state signatures as well as audit timestamp | ACCEPTED | A date-bound audit timestamp alone is too weak to detect same-day content drift, so the report needs a stronger freshness check against persisted findings state |\n| D-015 | 2026-04-12 | Apply waivers as an audit-time overlay rather than embedding waiver rules inside evaluators | ACCEPTED | Evaluators should keep emitting deterministic raw findings so the same checks can be reused with or without waivers, while audit assembly owns exception handling |\n| D-016 | 2026-04-12 | Centralise score calculation in a shared module and count only active unwaived findings against score | ACCEPTED | Score semantics must be documented, deterministic, and consistent across evaluators and audit assembly, and waived issues should not drag active score down |\n| D-017 | 2026-04-12 | Use one embedded fenced JSON review-evidence block as the shared contract for governance validation and historical review retrieval | ACCEPTED | One small structured block inside markdown keeps review evidence inspectable in git while avoiding two drifting review-data formats |\n| D-018 | 2026-04-12 | Keep historical review retrieval as explicit consult output rather than hiding it inside generic guidance text | ACCEPTED | Review history should be visible, bounded, and machine-readable so later consumers can use it without reparsing prose |\n| D-019 | 2026-04-18 | SVC-003 is declare-a-contract with a closed four-mode set (`mode.user_oidc`, `mode.service_rs256`, `mode.api_key`, `mode.bearer_legacy`); commit `66ba8a4` codifies `mode.user_oidc` with no rework; WP-SCP-018 `--auth-token` classifies as `mode.bearer_legacy` with deprecation close date 2026-06-30 | ACCEPTED | Estate reality has three live token types plus a cookie session flow; a single fixed mechanism breaks machine callers; a closed mode set prevents drift; codifying the existing CT OIDC layer keeps the in-repo auth story consistent without forcing a rework |\n| D-020 | 2026-04-18 | `_normalise_scope` accepts `request.scope.area_id` as the normaliser hint when `_infer_area_id` raises `AreaIdInferenceError`; mismatched-inference still raises the same way as before | ACCEPTED | Adopters auditing scopes that lack an ENH-style marker (e.g. a root-level `services.yml` dogfood) would otherwise have to plant placeholder enhancement docs to satisfy area-id inference; the narrow relaxation preserves the auditor discipline of catching inferred-vs-claimed mismatches while closing the genuine uninferrable-scope ergonomic gap |\n| D-022 | 2026-04-21 | Adopt reusable GitHub Actions workflow + OPA/Conftest + Renovate shared preset + required status check with `enforce_admins=true` as the estate's policy federation primitive. Alternatives rejected: `palantir/policy-bot` (GitHub App per org, HCL-not-Rego, no Renovate cascade, no native waiver-overlay); custom control-plane service (build cost, duplicates GH Actions + branch protection primitives) | ACCEPTED | Industry-standard shape; no custom control-plane service required; cheapest path converting SCP from advisory auditor to pre-merge gate; Renovate preset cascade gives \"change one rule → enforce across 10 repos in 24h\"; supports waiver-aware evaluation and structured annotation payloads. Full rationale body in `docs/plans/WP-SCP-020-policy-federation-primitive.md` §10 |\n| D-023 | 2026-04-21 | Reject multi-agent free-form chat-forum pattern as the agent-coordination venue; adopt structured proposal queue (`docs/reviews/proposals/PROP-NNN.md` + MCP `propose()` tool — initial stub in WP-SCP-021 slice 021E; full adjudication workflow in `WP-SCP-022-proposal-queue`, separately tracked per `docs/plans/WP-SCP-022-implementation-programme-plan.md` §12). Agents never free-form chat with each other; they read SCP's decision records and call SCP's MCP tools | ACCEPTED | Du et al. (ICML 2024, `arxiv 2305.14325`) show multi-agent debate converges to consensus fast including when the consensus is wrong; Anthropic's multi-agent research post reports ~15× token cost with compounding coordination errors; human-on-the-loop literature converges on moderator-as-bottleneck past a few decisions/hour. Proposal-queue pattern scales because the human is on the loop, not in it. Full rationale body in `docs/plans/WP-SCP-020-policy-federation-primitive.md` §10 (decision originator) and `docs/plans/WP-SCP-021-scp-as-mcp-server.md` §10 (`propose()` stub design). Note 2026-04-28: original D-023 forward-referenced \"WP-SCP-022 plan when that WP opens\"; WP-SCP-022 v0.2 is the implementation programme plan, not the proposal-queue WP — the rationale-body expansion lives in the parent plans named here, and the adjudication WP is renamed `WP-SCP-022-proposal-queue` |\n| D-024 | 2026-04-21 | Adopt Anthropic's Model Context Protocol (MCP) as the estate's agent-to-SCP tool protocol. Transports: stdio (primary, local, trust-the-process) + streamable HTTP (opt-in, bearer-token via `SCP_MCP_TOKEN` with 30-day rotation overlap). Ed25519 asymmetric signing for consult receipts — SCP holds private key; adopters verify with public key published at `scp://security/signing-keys` + committed `docs/security/mcp-signing-keys.pub` (90-day rotation overlap). Python `mcp` SDK pinned by version + sdist SHA256; distributed via PyPI as `standards-control-plane[mcp]` extra. OAuth deferred to SCP-075-oauth | ACCEPTED | MCP is the dominant vertical agent-to-tool protocol post Linux Foundation Agentic AI Foundation launch (Dec 2025); Anthropic is protocol origin; Claude Code/Desktop + ACC speak MCP natively. Asymmetric signing prevents adopter-held-key forgery (HMAC symmetric rejected in adversarial review round 1). Alternatives rejected: raw REST (no client ecosystem); custom JSON-RPC (reinvents MCP); gRPC (no agent client support); HMAC symmetric (forgeable by any key-holding adopter). Full rationale in `docs/plans/WP-SCP-021-scp-as-mcp-server.md` §10 |\n| D-025 | 2026-04-21 | Scoped pre-code consult requirement with Ed25519-signed receipts. Downstream `CLAUDE.md` mandates `scp.consult_rules` before code authoring in governed domains; Claude Code `PreCommit` hook refuses commit without valid receipt. Scope rule: hook consults `scp://rules/domain-map` via `resolve_domain(changed_files)`; non-empty result → receipt required; default-skip globs for docs-only changes. Receipt envelope binds `{schema_version, key_id, repo, head_sha, base_sha, changed_files_hash, domains_covered, issued_at, expires_at ≤ 2h}`; hook validates every field. Break-glass `SCP_MCP_ALLOW_OFFLINE=true` requires committed `docs/overrides/OVERRIDE-NNN.md` with schema-validated expiry ≤ 14d; emits tracked finding SCP-MCP-E010 | ACCEPTED | Client-side enforcement pairs with server-side gate (WP-SCP-020) as belt + braces; scoped + TTL + PR-bound receipts prevent replay/forgery/scope-bypass. Break-glass requires sibling OVERRIDE artefact so fail-closed posture is maintained. Rego gate is authoritative — MCP consult never bypasses. Full rationale in `docs/plans/WP-SCP-021-scp-as-mcp-server.md` §10 |\n| D-026 | 2026-04-28 | Autonomous-dispatch protocol for SCP implementation slices. Each WP-SCP-020 and WP-SCP-021 implementation slice flows through four-tier dispatch: Opus orchestrator (current agent) prepares dispatch package conforming to `~/Projects/acc/schemas/codex_work_package.schema.json`; Codex executor tier runs the slice via `codex_dispatch.py`; 3× parallel Sonnet R1 reviewers apply correctness / safety_bypass / completeness_governance lenses via `claude_dispatch.py` with 500 ms stagger; Opus consolidates verdicts and orchestrates fix rounds. Recurse to fixpoint per `feedback_recursive_adversarial_review.md`. Fix-round budget 5; on budget exhaustion, slice escalates to user review. Evidence persists at `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/<slice-id>/`. No slice merges without 3× APPROVED verdict | ACCEPTED | Estate four-tier dispatch standard since 2026-04-22 (per `feedback_four_tier_dispatch.md`); applies to non-trivial work. SCP implementation slices are non-trivial — they touch CI, branch protection, signing keys, and PyPI. Adversarial review pattern proven on WP-SCP-019 (5 rounds), WP-SCP-020 (5 rounds), WP-SCP-021 (3 rounds, fixpoint). Persisting evidence in-repo gives a permanent audit trail and lets future agents recover from session-bounded amnesia. Full rationale in `docs/plans/WP-SCP-022-implementation-programme-plan.md` §4 |\n| D-027 | 2026-04-28 | Parallel-track execution for WP-SCP-020 (federation primitive) and WP-SCP-021 (MCP server) implementation, Scope Y per user 2026-04-28 selection. Both tracks run concurrently from dispatch-zero to user-gate. Track 1 (020): 020B → 020B.1 → 020B.2 → 020C → 020C.1 → 020J → 020K → 020D1 → 020H part 1 → 020E.a → [USER-GATE-A0 release sign-off] → 020H part 2 → 020D2 → [USER-GATE-A] (12 implementation slices + 1 mid-chain human-input gate + 1 terminal gate, matching WP-SCP-020 §3 canonical sequence verbatim). Track 2 (021): 021B → 021C → 021D → 021E → [USER-GATE-C] (4 implementation slices + 1 terminal gate). Concurrent-dispatch limit: 4 active Codex sessions across both tracks. `pyproject.toml` partition: Track 1 owns `[tool.scp.federation]` and adds `scp-policy-check` to `[project.scripts]`; Track 2 owns `[project.optional-dependencies.mcp]` and adds `scp-mcp-server` to `[project.scripts]`. `docs/STATUS.md` excluded from all slice scope_boundary globs | ACCEPTED | WP-SCP-021 §scope statement declares the tracks independent; serial execution would defer MCP availability for ~2× wall-clock without quality gain. Concurrent dispatch within rate-limit envelope per `four-tier-dispatch-field-report-2026-04-22.md`. Full rationale in `docs/plans/WP-SCP-022-implementation-programme-plan.md` §4.4. Track 1 sequence corrected in v0.2 (R1 fix-round) to match WP-SCP-020 §3 canonical ordering — earlier draft omitted 020K and inverted 020D2 placement |\n| D-028 | 2026-04-28 | SCP adopts D-048 / ADR-016 Design Parity Build Method (DPBM) as a contract input for any SCP slice that produces designed visual output. Layered amendment to four-tier dispatch — prototype + flows + design principles enter the contract layer the build is bound to. Enforcement-injection mechanism per `docs/plans/WP-SCP-022-implementation-programme-plan.md` §4.6: visual-output slices' dispatch packages MUST include a \"DPBM contract\" subsection naming prototype path, flow diagram, and design-principles reference; orchestrator verifies subsection presence before launching the review pass; absence is treated as a CRIT finding. Within WP-SCP-022 itself no slices match the DPBM \"applies\" criteria (workflow / Rego / MCP-server / CI work, no visual output); D-028 is forward-looking. WP-SCP-024 estate cascade inherits D-028; FLA, ACC, RI canary-onboarding instructions reference the DPBM contract layer. D-028 lands operationally once CT PR #202 + ACC PR #106 have merged | ACCEPTED | DPBM closes a structural gap that pure-code lenses can't see — a frontend built under standard four-tier dispatch can satisfy every test and still drift from the design. Without an SCP-side adoption decision, agents working through SCP's MCP consult won't be told that the contract for visual-output work includes design artefacts. CT PR #202 + ACC PR #106 (both 2026-04-28) introduce the upstream doctrine; SCP adopts at the policy layer. Full rationale + injection-mechanism specification in `docs/plans/WP-SCP-022-implementation-programme-plan.md` §4.6 |\n| D-029 | 2026-04-29 | `policy-check.yml` reusable workflow `permissions:` block expands to `contents: read, statuses: write` to support WP-SCP-022 020C.1(vi) read-back commit-status posting on the sibling context `scp/policy-check-readback`. Original 020B(iii) baseline was `contents: read` only. The `statuses: write` privilege uses the caller's `${{ github.token }}` and posts only to `repos/<caller>/statuses/${{ pull_request.head.sha }}`; no other write surface is unlocked. Disabling the read-back would require accepting a regression on 020C.1(vi); keeping `contents: read` only would prevent the read-back from being posted | ACCEPTED | The read-back surface is the only place a PR reviewer sees waiver/disabled-rule posture without reading JSON artefacts; WP-SCP-020 §4.3 fixpoint criteria for 020C.1(vi) require it. The privilege footprint is narrowest possible — `statuses: write` does not allow content modification, branch operations, issue/PR comments, or token impersonation. The workflow checkout uses `persist-credentials: false` so the elevated privilege is bounded to the policy-check job. Plan amendment: `docs/plans/WP-SCP-020-policy-federation-primitive.md` §4 020B(iii) updated with cross-reference to D-029. Identified by WP-SCP-022 R1 completeness review (MAJ-005) |\n| D-030 | 2026-04-30 | Apply WP-SCP-020 slice 020J on the SCP repository: (a) tag-protection rule on `v*` (legacy tag-protection API and/or Repository Ruleset) preventing force-push or deletion of release tags; (b) `required-signed-commits` on `main` so every commit reaching `main` carries a verified signature. Both are preconditions for slice 020D1 (self-dogfood wrapper pinned at the v1.0.0-rc.1 commit-SHA) and 020D2 (required-status-check + v1.0.0 cut). Configured via `scripts/configure-020j-protections.sh` (idempotent API caller). Documented in `docs/security/branch-protection.md`. Bus-factor-1 acknowledged: a single GitHub admin (@jrnb2024) can disable these via repo Settings; quarterly review cadence per WP-SCP-020 §14 U-sec-2 closure | ACCEPTED | Path (a) selected at WP-SCP-020 plan merge per §14 U-sec-2 — Personal GitHub Pro tier supports both protections natively, no signed-release-manifest substitute needed. Tag-protection prevents tag mutation post-cut (the supply-chain anchor for adopters pinning by SHA + comment-tag); required-signed-commits prevents un-attested code from reaching the gate's source of truth. Future direct commits to main without a verified signature are blocked, which is the intent (forces all changes through PRs whose squash-merges are auto-signed by GitHub's bot key). The unsigned commit `bcfc706` (USER-GATE-C signoff, 2026-04-29, unsigned) predates this enablement and is grandfathered. Identified as gate-readiness work in `docs/plans/WP-SCP-020-policy-federation-primitive.md` §4 020J + §3 sequence + §8 risks (\"Bootstrap trust\") + §9.1 sequencing |\n| D-031 | 2026-04-30 | Apply WP-SCP-020 slice 020K on the SCP repository in the personal-account / single-operator mode resolved at §14 U-k closure: (a) NO `scp-break-glass` GitHub team is created — GitHub teams are not available on user namespaces and `jrnb2024` is a personal user account; (b) `CODEOWNERS` extends to cover the additional governance-sensitive paths named in §4 020K (canonical-minimum), then broadened/extended via 020K R1+R2+R3 safety reviews to close 7 MAJ + 4 MIN + 6 nit findings — see CODEOWNERS file header + `FIX-ROUND-{1,2,3}.md` for the full audit trail of which paths were broadened (subsuming a narrower canonical rule) vs added net-new; (c) at slice 020D2, branch-protection on `main` is configured with `required_approving_review_count = 1`, `dismiss_stale_reviews: true`, and `require_review_from_non_author = false` (solo operator; self-approval is the only option) — **NOTE (D-033 amendment, 2026-04-30 evening):** the anticipated `required_approving_review_count = 1` was corrected to `count = 0` by D-033 when the first PR through enforced mode revealed that GitHub forbids PR self-review; count=1 + sole CODEOWNER = operator locked out of repo. See D-033 for the live state, the operational test path (USER-GATE-A signoff PR #64), and the second-maintainer escalation path; (d) §8 bus-factor-1 risk row remains LIVE with quarterly escalation review at 2026-07-21 named in `STATUS.md` \"Open scheduled follow-ups\" and in auto-memory `project_scheduled_followups.md`. **Escalation path when a second maintainer onboards:** add them to `CODEOWNERS`, flip `require_review_from_non_author = true`, amend §8 risk row to CLOSED. **020D1 → 020D2 dependency:** 020K completes before 020D2 opens (the branch-protection wiring at 020D2 reads from CODEOWNERS established here). Identified at WP-SCP-020 plan §3 sequence + §4 020K + §14 U-k closure | ACCEPTED | Personal-account path is the only viable shape for v1.0.0 on a personal user namespace. The CODEOWNERS expansion forces every future change to `renovate/**` (Renovate preset that cascades pin-bumps across adopters), `docs/DECISIONS.md` (the decision-record canonical home), and `output/findings/waivers.json` (the safety-valve store) through reviewer attention rather than allowing direct push. Bus-factor-1 is acknowledged as live operational exposure rather than papered over: `require_review_from_non_author = false` reflects reality (a solo operator cannot self-approve under non-author review), and the 2026-07-21 quarterly review is the named re-evaluation moment. Path (b) extension of `CODEOWNERS` is forward-looking on `renovate/**` (the directory does not yet exist in the working tree; it lands in slice 020F); CODEOWNERS rules on non-existent paths are no-ops, so adding the rule pre-emptively is harmless and removes a future drift risk where 020F could land without simultaneously adding the matching CODEOWNERS row. Full rationale in `docs/plans/WP-SCP-020-policy-federation-primitive.md` §4 020K + §8 (bus factor risk row, line 188) + §14 U-k closure |\n| D-032 | 2026-04-30 | Apply WP-SCP-020 slice 020D2 on the SCP repository post-USER-GATE-A0: enable `scp/policy-check` as a required status check on `main` with `strict: true` (PR must be up-to-date with base before evaluation); set `enforce_admins: true` (no admin bypass); set `required_pull_request_reviews` with `required_approving_review_count: 1`, `dismiss_stale_reviews: true`, `require_code_owner_reviews: true`; explicitly leave `require_review_from_non_author: false` per 020K personal-account closure (single-operator self-approval is the only viable path). Configured via `scripts/configure-020d2-required-check.sh` (idempotent). After 020D2, every PR to `main` must pass the federation primitive's gate AND have at least one approving review AND have a verified signature on every commit AND cannot be bypassed by the admin role. The 020E.a pre-protection canary (workflow-run-id 25172373569) demonstrated the deny path on a real PR with a real violation pre-flip; 020E.b will demonstrate that the now-required check actually blocks merge. Bus-factor-1 acknowledged: a single GitHub admin (@jrnb2024) is the operator; quarterly review at 2026-07-21 (per 020K + D-031) reconciles documented-vs-live state. **Note (D-033 forward-reference, 2026-04-30 evening):** the live state of three D-032 parameters was amended same-day by D-033 — the required-check context name corrected from `scp/policy-check` to `policy-check / scp/policy-check` (rendered Actions name); `required_approving_review_count` corrected from 1 to 0 (single-operator self-approval is structurally impossible on GitHub); `require_code_owner_reviews` corrected from true to false (same root cause). See D-033 for the operational test path that surfaced these | ACCEPTED | This is the operational closure of the WP-SCP-020 federation primitive — SCP gates itself on its own `main`. Closure of governance B-2 (the original brief was \"convert SCP from advisory auditor to deterministic pre-merge enforcement\"). Required-status-check + enforce_admins=true + required-signed-commits + required-PR-reviews compose into a 4-layer protection: (1) every commit is cryptographically attested, (2) every change runs through the SCP gate, (3) every change has at least one human approval, (4) admin override is OFF. Single-operator self-approval is the bus-factor-1 cost; the 2026-07-21 escalation review is the named re-evaluation moment. The pre-protection canary (020E.a) proved the deny mechanism worked on real PR; flipping to required adds enforcement, not behaviour. Identified at WP-SCP-020 plan §3 sequence + §4 020D2; preconditions 020J + 020K + 020D1 + 020E.a + USER-GATE-A0 all closed before this slice opens |\n| D-033 | 2026-04-30 | WP-SCP-022 020D2.1 reconciliation: amend D-032's branch-protection shape on the SCP repo to reflect two operational realities surfaced when the first PR (USER-GATE-A signoff PR #64) attempted to merge through enforced mode. (a) **Required-check context name correction:** WP-SCP-020 020B(xi) specified `scp/policy-check` as the context name. GitHub Actions renders the actual check-run as `<wrapper-workflow-name> / <reusable-job-name>` — for SCP self this is `policy-check / scp/policy-check`. The bare `scp/policy-check` context matches only the readback status posted by the reusable workflow's commit-status step, NOT the primary check-run from the wrapper. Required-check context updated to `policy-check / scp/policy-check`. (b) **Personal-account review-shape correction:** D-032 originally set `required_approving_review_count = 1` + `require_code_owner_reviews = true` per WP-SCP-020 020D2 spec. GitHub forbids PR authors from approving their own PRs. In single-operator mode with @jrnb2024 as the only maintainer AND only CODEOWNER, count=1 + codeowner=true locks the operator out of every PR; `enforce_admins=true` prevents `gh pr merge --admin` from bypassing. Updated to `count=0` + `codeowner_reviews=false` in single-operator mode. CODEOWNERS still serves as documentation of who SHOULD approve when a second maintainer arrives. Forward-looking discipline preserved: when a second maintainer onboards, flip count=1 + codeowner=true + non-author=true in one operation. Both corrections are landed in `scripts/configure-020d2-required-check.sh` (idempotent re-run reproduces the live state) + `docs/security/branch-protection.md` + WP-SCP-020 §8 risk row. The 2026-07-21 quarterly escalation review re-evaluates the personal-account shape | ACCEPTED | First-PR-through-enforcement is the operational test of the spec; the test surfaced two assumptions that didn't survive contact with GitHub's actual behaviour: (1) the bare context name from 020B(xi) was incomplete (didn't anticipate Actions' `<workflow> / <job>` rendering for reusable-workflow callers), and (2) \"self-approval is the only option\" in 020K's wording was operationally impossible (GitHub forbids PR self-review). Both corrections are operational closure of the spec gap; neither weakens the security posture. The 4-layer protection (signed commits + required check + no admin bypass + tag protection) remains intact. The review-count=0 acceptance is the explicit bus-factor-1 cost; review enforcement returns the moment a second maintainer joins. Identified by USER-GATE-A signoff PR #64's failed merge attempt 2026-04-30 |\n| D-034 | 2026-04-30 | Apply WP-SCP-020 slice 020F renovate-tag-protection (parallel ruleset to D-030's `v*` ruleset, targeting `refs/tags/renovate/v*`). Closes 020F R1 safety review CRIT-SAFE-001: `renovate/v*` tag series unprotected — an attacker with push access could silently re-point `renovate/v1.0.0` to a malicious commit and cascade adversarial Renovate config to every estate adopter on their next scheduled run. The 020J ruleset matches `refs/tags/v*` (literal v-prefix at start) only. New ruleset `scp-tag-protection-renovate-v` applies same protections (deletion / non_fast_forward / update blocked, `enforcement: active`, `bypass_actors: []`). Configured via `scripts/configure-020f-renovate-tag-protection.sh` (idempotent, jq-based JSON construction to eliminate heredoc-interpolation injection surface, multi-property post-creation verification). Documented in `docs/security/branch-protection.md` \"Tag protection — renovate/v* pattern\" section. Symmetric with D-030 in posture and rationale; the only material difference is the tag-pattern segment. The `renovate/v*` series carries equal supply-chain weight as `v*` because every adopter that extends the preset cascades changes from the renovate-pattern tag to their own gate config | ACCEPTED | The 020J ruleset's `v*` literal-prefix pattern is by-design narrow; broadening to all release-track tags would require a separate ruleset per pattern. Renovate's preset shorthand `github>jrnb2024/standards-control-plane-//renovate/default#renovate/v1.0.0` is the standard Renovate adopter onboarding shape; without ruleset protection, the entire preset trust chain is rooted in mutable tag refs. Bus-factor-1 acknowledged identically to D-030: the same admin who owns @jrnb2024 is the one publishing tags; the protection guards against accidental force-push, not against malicious admin action. Quarterly review cadence (2026-07-30 in STATUS.md \"Open scheduled follow-ups\") covers both rulesets together. Identified by 020F R1 safety review (workflow run dispatched 2026-04-30 evening) — surfaced under the no-silent-descope governance invariant after fix-round-1 closed the substantive R1 findings |\n| D-035 | 2026-04-30 | Adopt the WP-SCP-020 slice 020G adopter-helper invocation procedure as estate doctrine. The required-check context name `policy-check / scp/policy-check` traces to D-033 (the rendered Actions check-run name established for the SCP self-dogfood gate). Adopters of the SCP federation primitive enable this required status check on their default branch via `scripts/enable-required-check.sh` from the SCP repo. The script applies branch protection in two API calls (unified PUT for status-checks/admins/reviews + dedicated POST to `required_signatures` sub-resource per the GitHub API shape), refuses to bypass admin enforcement without an explicit `--i-understand-this-bypasses-the-gate` acknowledgement flag, refuses to run in CI (CI=true / GITHUB_ACTIONS=true env-var guards), preserves any pre-existing `required_pull_request_reviews` shape rather than nulling it, and emits an invocation-log block (operator, timestamp, script SHA256 + git SHA, target repo+branch, before/after API JSON) for paste into `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md`. The log commit is part of the invocation procedure: an apply without the log entry is considered unrecorded. Estate cascade rollout (WP-SCP-024) drives this script across PIM, recommender, shopify-app, mapp-doc-agent, control-tower; FLA pilot (WP-SCP-020.1) is the canary first invocation | ACCEPTED | Symmetric posture with D-030 (020J `v*` tag-protection), D-031 (020K CODEOWNERS personal-account), D-032 (020D2 SCP-self required-status-check), D-034 (020F `renovate/v*` tag-protection): every operational procedure that mutates third-party repository state ships an idempotent applier + ratifying D-NNN + invocation log convention. The bootstrap-only / no-CI guard reflects that this script writes to a third-party repo's branch protection — an automation tool that ran it unattended could mass-mutate the estate via stolen credentials before any operator noticed. The `--i-understand-this-bypasses-the-gate` confirmation reflects that `--no-enforce-admins` defeats the whole point of the federation primitive on the target repo and so should require explicit intent. The `required_pull_request_reviews` preservation reflects that adopters with multi-maintainer teams have legitimate review-shape state that 020G must not destroy. Identified at WP-SCP-020 §4 020G + 020G R1 review (3 MAJ + 5 MIN + 2 nit on safety, 2 MAJ + 4 MIN + nit on completeness, 3 MAJ + 2 MIN + 2 nit on correctness — fixpoint via fix-round-1) |\n| D-036 | 2026-05-01 | Adopt `policies/VERSIONING.md` MAJOR/MINOR/PATCH semver contract on the SCP federation primitive's public surfaces (`workflow_call.inputs` on `policy-check.yml`, `schemas/policy-check-summary.schema.json` + `schemas/rule-config.schema.json`, `SCP-R-NNN` rule IDs) WITH a one-release deprecation ramp before any breaking change AND `docs/reviews/rule-proposals/` RFC-lite process (quorum=1 SCP-CODEOWNER per D-031 single-operator mode; 48h wall-clock window with weekend-counts; 0 approvals = auto-defer with `defer` label; bypass-introducing proposals require explicit §5 enumeration and a non-waivable 48h window per 020H.1 R1 SAFE-MAJ-001 closure) as estate doctrine for SCP federation primitive versioning and rule governance. Closes WP-SCP-020 §4 020H.1 (i)+(ii)+(iii) and BS-5. Forward-compat to 2-of-N quorum when a second maintainer onboards (D-031 escalation path, 2026-07-21 review). Internal-only surfaces (`lib/policy_check_invocation.sh`, `scripts/scp-policy-check.lock`, helper scripts in `scripts/`, Renovate preset internal shape, OPA Rego helpers in `policies/scp_common.rego`) are explicitly NOT covered — they may be refactored without notice | ACCEPTED | Symmetric posture with D-035 (020G adopter-helper invocation): every new governance procedure binding adopter behaviour ships a ratifying D-NNN row to make the doctrine searchable in the canonical decision-record home rather than buried in a slice DISPATCH-NOTE. The one-release deprecation ramp is the MINIMUM version-skew adopters must tolerate before a surface vanishes; combined with the Renovate preset's automated SHA-pin bumps, this gives every adopter at least one full PR cycle's worth of warning annotations before a breaking change lands. The RFC-lite process is intentionally lightweight at v1.0.0 because the SCP estate is single-operator; the auto-defer mechanism is currently a manual close + label step. As estate scale grows (WP-SCP-024 cascade), the auto-defer mechanism may be lifted to a GitHub Action that runs on a schedule. Identified at WP-SCP-020 §4 020H.1 plan row + 020H.1 R1 completeness COMP-MAJ-003 closure |\n| D-037 | 2026-05-01 | Grant `issues: write` permission to the `release-gate-violation-issue` job in `.github/workflows/release-gate.yml` (per WP-SCP-022 020H.3.1, closing TF-020H3rg-003). Privilege scope is the JOB level (not workflow default); the release-gate evaluation job continues to run with `contents: read` only. The new job fires only on `needs.release-gate.result == 'failure' && github.event_name == 'push'` — workflow_dispatch dry-runs and clean push:tags runs never reach it. The job's only mutation is `gh issue create` / `gh issue comment` / `gh label create --force` against the SCP source repo's own Issues namespace; no other write surface is unlocked. The auto-opened issue assigns `@jrnb2024` (single-operator mode per D-031); forward-compat env `SCP_RELEASE_GATE_ASSIGNEES` for 2nd-maintainer escalation. Symmetric with D-029 (020C.1 `statuses: write` for read-back commit-status) — a privilege-write expansion ratified by an amending decision row that names the precise threat-surface and bounded behaviour | ACCEPTED | The release-gate's last-line-of-defense alarm needs SOME write surface to surface beyond the workflow log; opening a GitHub issue is the lightest-weight signal that survives operator absence (the workflow log is ephemeral; the issue persists + assigns + sends a notification). Privilege-of-least-privilege is honoured by job-level scoping rather than workflow-level — the gate-evaluation steps (pip install, schema validate, ramp check, expired-config check) cannot accidentally open issues because they run in the release-gate job which has only `contents: read`. CODEOWNERS coverage on `.github/**` (per WP-SCP-020 020K) gates any change to release-gate.yml itself. The forward-compat env mirrors `SCP_REGRESSION_ASSIGNEES` from canary-replay.yml. Identified at WP-SCP-022 020H.3.1 R1 safety SAFE-MIN-003 + SAFE-MAJ-001 closures |\n| D-038 | 2026-05-02 | Adopt hash-pinned Python supply-chain installs across the SCP federation primitive's two YAML/JSON-validating workflows (`.github/workflows/policy-check.yml` × 4 install sites, `.github/workflows/release-gate.yml` × 1 install site) via `pip install --require-hashes -r requirements/policy-check.txt`. Top-level pins (`pyyaml==6.0.2` + `jsonschema==4.23.0`) match the prior inline pins exactly — no version bump on the public Python deps; only the install mechanism is hardened. Lockfile generated by `pip-compile --generate-hashes` from `requirements/policy-check.in`; full transitive closure (`attrs`, `jsonschema-specifications`, `referencing`, `rpds-py`) hash-pinned with all-platform wheel SHA256s. Closes WP-SCP-022 TF-020H3rg-002 (filed at 020H.3 R1 safety MIN-SAFE-006 — a PyPI compromise of either pinned version could substitute a malicious package on the next install; `--require-hashes` causes pip to refuse install when the served wheel's SHA256 is not in the lockfile). PATCH bump (v1.0.0 → v1.0.1) per `policies/VERSIONING.md` PATCH category (\"internal refactors of helpers/lib\"); no `policies/deprecations.yaml` entry required (no surface deprecation, no behaviour change for adopters). Lockfile regeneration command captured in `requirements/policy-check.txt` header AND `requirements/policy-check.in` documentation block | ACCEPTED | Symmetric supply-chain hardening with the pre-existing `scripts/scp-policy-check.lock` (OPA + Conftest + Regal binary SHA256 verification) and Sigstore attestation soft-warn for OPA (TF-007). All three layers — Python deps, OPA-family binaries, GitHub Actions SHA pins — are now hash-anchored at SCP source. The lockfile + `.in` source pair mirrors `scripts/.tool-versions` + `scripts/scp-policy-check.lock` (input + lockfile pattern). Bus-factor-1 acknowledged for lockfile regeneration: an operator who regenerates without re-reviewing the pip-compile output could introduce drifted transitive versions; mitigation is the `requirements/policy-check.in` source-of-truth (pins only the top-level deps; transitive resolution is automatic but reviewable in the diff). Future upgrades to `pyyaml` / `jsonschema` itself follow the same path: edit `.in`, regenerate `.txt`, review diff. The Sigstore re-tightening (TF-007) is parallel work on a different supply-chain dimension. Identified at 020H.3 R1 safety MIN-SAFE-006 closure |\n| D-039 | 2026-05-02 | Extend the slice 020M hash-pinned Python supply-chain install pattern to the SCP-self conflict-gate workflow (`.github/workflows/conflict-gate.yml` \"Install Python dependencies\" step) per WP-SCP-022 slice 020N, closing TF-020M-001. New `requirements/conflict-gate.in` (top-level pins matching `pyproject.toml` `[project.dependencies]` + `[project.optional-dependencies.dev]`: fastapi, uvicorn, httpx, jsonschema, pydantic, pyyaml, pytest, ruff, mypy) + generated `requirements/conflict-gate.txt` (pip-compile --generate-hashes under Python 3.12 to match `setup-python: python-version: \"3.12\"`; full transitive closure with all-platform wheel SHA256s). Install pattern: (i) `pip install --require-hashes -r requirements/conflict-gate.txt` (PyPI deps); (ii) `pip install --no-deps vendor/python/ct_auth-0.8.0-py3-none-any.whl` (local vendored wheel — not on PyPI; CODEOWNERS-protected via `vendor/** @jrnb2024`); (iii) `pip install --no-deps -e .` (editable local SCP package without re-resolving deps already hash-verified in step (i)); (iv) post-install version-pin assertion (`assert yaml.__version__ == \"6.0.3\"; assert jsonschema.__version__ == \"4.26.0\"; assert fastapi.__version__ == \"0.136.1\"; assert pydantic.VERSION == \"2.13.3\"`) defending against future-PR drift (mirrors 020M SAFE-MAJ-002 pattern). Deliberate pyyaml-version drift accepted: conflict-gate.txt resolves pyyaml==6.0.3, policy-check.txt pins pyyaml==6.0.2 — independent workflows with independent supply-chain anchors; both hash-anchored. **No semver bump.** Per `policies/VERSIONING.md` §\"Scope\", `conflict-gate.yml` is not in the public-surface enumeration (workflow_call inputs on `policy-check.yml`, schema contracts, rule IDs); it is a SCP-self-only workflow that adopters do not consume. Therefore no `policies/deprecations.yaml` entry, no version-manifest bump, no release notes — internal hardening | ACCEPTED | Symmetric posture with D-038 (slice 020M hash-pinning of policy-check.yml + release-gate.yml). The conflict-gate is the third Python-installing workflow in the SCP repo; closing the unhardened install completes the Python supply-chain hardening across all three SCP-self workflows that pip-install at runtime. Threat surface (PyPI compromise of conflict-gate's deps) was acknowledged at 020M R1 safety as MIN-class because conflict-gate is SCP-self-only (not adopter-facing); a compromise would degrade SCP self's policy-evaluator quality but not directly compromise adopters. The user's directive to clear all outstanding TFs (rather than defer to v1.1 maintenance) raised this from forward-compat to in-flight closure. The `--no-deps` editable install of the local SCP package is the tradeoff: pip refuses `--require-hashes` on editable installs, so the local source tree is installed without re-resolving (its PyPI deps are already hash-verified above). The `ct-auth` vendored wheel is similarly outside `--require-hashes` (pip refuses for local file: URLs); chain-of-trust is the `vendor/**` CODEOWNERS coverage + the wheel being in-tree (no network fetch). Identified at 020M R1 safety SAFE-MIN-003 closure |\n| D-040 | 2026-05-02 | Amend the rule-RFC process (`docs/reviews/rule-proposals/README.md` §Process step 2) so that the 48h wall-clock review window is a CEILING (auto-defer trigger) rather than a FLOOR (minimum wait time) **in single-operator mode** (D-031 default at v1.0.0). Rationale: in single-operator mode the proposal author IS the only `SCP-CODEOWNERS` member; there are no external reviewers for the wall-clock pause to enable. The recursive 3-lens R1/R2 adversarial review (per `feedback_recursive_adversarial_review.md`) IS the adversarial-review surface; the wall-clock pause was vestigial theatre that produced no additional safety. The operator MAY merge a proposal as soon as adversarial review reaches fixpoint (no new CRIT/MAJ on a complete cycle) AND the proposal PR is open with the `scp-rule-proposal` label applied. The 48h CEILING remains in force: zero approvals by 48h still triggers auto-defer (`defer` label per slice 020L label-provisioning). When a second maintainer onboards (D-031 escalation, 2026-07-21 review), the 48h becomes a mandatory MINIMUM to give the second maintainer time to review — the CEILING-not-FLOOR posture is single-operator-only. Bypass-introducing proposals (`Bypass-surface non-empty: true`) retain their non-waivable 48h MINIMUM regardless of operator-count posture, because the additional adversarial-review value of a wall-clock pause IS material when a new bypass surface is being introduced (the operator may catch their own bypass-introducing PR's flaws on second look more reliably than for a non-bypass additive rule). Surfaced by 020L dogfood (the first RFC actually walked through the process). Closes WP-SCP-022 020L post-R2 process-gap | ACCEPTED | The 020L dogfood surfaced this gap operationally: the operator (single-maintainer) reached R1+R2 fixpoint on a non-bypass proposal and was about to wait 48h before merging — at which point the wait was identified as theatre rather than safety (no second pair of eyes to chime in). Theatre-vs-safety is the doctrine question the dogfood is designed to surface cheaply. The amendment narrows the discretionary posture to single-operator + non-bypass + post-fixpoint, leaving every other shape of the 48h window unchanged: bypass-introducing proposals keep the non-waivable minimum (the threat-surface justifies the wait); the post-2nd-maintainer-onboard 48h becomes mandatory minimum so the second maintainer has time to review; the 48h CEILING for auto-defer remains so abandoned proposals are surfaced. Symmetric with D-033's \"operational reality must amend the spec when the spec encounters a constraint that wasn't anticipated\" pattern. Identified at WP-SCP-022 slice 020L post-R2 fixpoint by operator (`@jrnb2024`) when reviewing the merge-readiness of PR #89 |\n| D-041 | 2026-05-03 | Adopt the cross-repo scorecard data shape (`schemas/scorecard-emit.schema.json` v0.1) + opt-in adopter participation model per WP-SCP-023 023B (this PR). The schema defines aggregated per-rule counts (raw_findings, denies, waived, rule_config_disabled), waivers aggregate (active_count, by_rule_id, expiring_within_30d), rule-config aggregate (disabled_rules, expiring_within_30d), repo / ref / commit / scp_version / RFC3339 emitted_at, and a verdict (allow/deny/warn). The schema EXPLICITLY DOES NOT include waiver content (`reason`, `approved_by`, `waiver_id` strings) — invariant 2 of WP-SCP-023 plan-doc binds this. The reusable workflow gains a `scorecard-emit: bool` `workflow_call` input (default false) so existing adopters see no behaviour change; opt-in adopters set `with: scorecard-emit: true`. Schema versioning: MAJOR-pinned per `policies/VERSIONING.md` D-036 + WP-SCP-023 plan-doc §10 Q1 — aggregator drops emits with unknown `schema_version`. Verification: GitHub Actions Artifact Attestation (OIDC-based) via `actions/attest-build-provenance@v4.1.0` (SHA `a2bbfa25...`) at emit time; aggregator verifies via `gh attestation verify --signer-workflow` (per D-042 to be filed at slice 023C). v1.2.0 cut on this slice's merge per VERSIONING.md MINOR category | ACCEPTED | This is the first slice of WP-SCP-023's implementation chain (023A plan-doc landed at PR #97, commit `7075234`, 2026-05-03 R4 fixpoint). The data shape was specified at plan-doc level; this slice ratifies it as code surface. Default-false on the workflow input is the load-bearing safety property: existing adopters cannot have new behaviour spring on them; opt-in is explicit per invariant 4. The waiver-content exclusion (invariant 2) is enforced by the schema's `additionalProperties: false` constraint on every level + the explicit field enumeration. The MAJOR-pinning posture means schema breakage requires v2.0.0 + adopter migration; v0.1 prefix on `schema_version` reflects the freedom to amend until first adopter opts in. Bus-factor-1 acknowledged: aggregator runs on the SCP repo with single-operator identity (D-031); 2026-07-21 quarterly review covers. Identified at WP-SCP-023 023A plan-doc §9 D-041 reservation |\n| D-042 | 2026-05-03 | Adopt the cross-repo scorecard aggregator pipeline trust model per WP-SCP-023 023C (this PR). Mechanism: per-emit verification via `gh attestation verify --signer-workflow <expected_workflow_ref>` (the GitHub Actions Artifact Attestation OIDC pattern, mandatory per the D-042 reservation note above + 023A R2 MAJ-SAFE-R2-001 closure); index trust rooted in git's required-signed-commits (slice 020J) + `output/scorecards/** @jrnb2024` CODEOWNERS coverage (slice 023A) + branch protection on main (slice 020D2), NOT a separate Ed25519 key layer. The aggregator runs as `.github/workflows/scorecard-aggregator.yml` on a weekly cron (Monday 06:00 UTC) + `workflow_dispatch`; two-job structure (aggregate read-only + commit write-scoped via job-level permissions, mirroring the 023B `attest-scorecard` job split); reads `docs/scorecards/opt-in-registry.yaml` (CODEOWNERS-protected at `docs/scorecards/**`), pulls each adopter's latest green policy-check run's `scorecard-emit.json` artifact, verifies attestation, schema-validates emit, aggregates into `output/scorecards/index.json` (schema: `schemas/scorecard-index.schema.json`), and opens a PR for operator review. Failure modes (verification_failure / unreachable / no_emit) record loudly per-adopter — never silently dropped. NEVER carries waiver content (invariant 2 enforced by schema `additionalProperties: false` at every level). Internal-only workflow per `policies/VERSIONING.md` §\"Scope\" (mirrors 020N conflict-gate.yml posture); no semver bump. Verifies WP-SCP-023 plan-doc invariant 6 (\"Aggregator output is Ed25519-signed\") in spirit — Sigstore/Fulcio uses Ed25519 under the hood, managed by GitHub rather than by an SCP-side private key, eliminating the private-key-distribution surface | ACCEPTED | Symmetric posture with D-041 (per-emit data shape + opt-in model) — D-041 ratified the EMITTER side of the trust model; D-042 ratifies the AGGREGATOR / VERIFIER side. The mandatory `--signer-workflow` constraint was already pre-committed at the D-042 reservation note above (closes 023A R2 MAJ-SAFE-R2-001) — this slice ratifies it as workflow code. The Ed25519-via-Sigstore framing eliminates the chicken-and-egg of \"where does the SCP private key live and how does the aggregator hold it without exposing adopters\" — GitHub manages the keys via OIDC + Sigstore. The two-job permission split (aggregate read-only + commit write-scoped) is identical to 023B's `policy-check` + `attest-scorecard` split (closes the analogous threat surface where workflow-level write permissions could be exploited by a compromised aggregate-side step). Bus-factor-1 acknowledged: aggregator runs on the SCP repo with single-operator identity (D-031); 2026-07-21 quarterly review covers. Identified at WP-SCP-023 023A plan-doc §9 D-042 reservation + 023A R2 MAJ-SAFE-R2-001 closure |\n| D-043 | 2026-05-03 | Adopt the cross-repo scorecard exposure surface — markdown report (`docs/scorecards/<YYYY-MM-DD>.md`) + MCP method `scp.consult_scorecard` — per WP-SCP-023 023D (this PR). Markdown generator at `scripts/generate-scorecard-report.py` produces deterministic golden-file-tested output reading `output/scorecards/index.json`; aggregator workflow `.github/workflows/scorecard-aggregator.yml` extended in the commit job to invoke the generator + add the rendered markdown to the operator-review PR alongside the index. MCP method `scp.consult_scorecard` (registered in `src/standards_control_plane/mcp_server/tools.py`): read-only contract per WP-SCP-023 plan-doc invariant 5; request shape `{repo_filter?, since_emitted_at?}`; response shape excludes waiver content (`reason` / `approved_by` / `waiver_id` — invariant 2) by Pydantic model construction (extra=forbid + explicit field whitelist). The MCP method reads `output/scorecards/index.json` from the SCP repo working copy at call time; staleness is observable via the response's `aggregated_at` field. ADOPT-001 §12.7.15 documents the adopter onboarding workflow. NEVER carries waiver content (invariant 2 enforced by Pydantic models + schema + dedicated pytest scans on both markdown + MCP responses) | ACCEPTED | Symmetric posture with D-041 + D-042 — D-043 ratifies the CONSUMER side: markdown for operators/auditors, MCP for agents/tooling. Read-only contract + Pydantic-enforced field whitelist + golden-file pytest together preserve invariant 2 across both surfaces. The MCP method does NOT introduce a new authentication primitive — it rides on WP-SCP-021's existing Ed25519 receipt mechanism (consult-class semantics). The markdown report is informational and CODEOWNERS-protected via `docs/scorecards/** @jrnb2024`; operators can hand-edit before merging the aggregator PR if needed (regenerator overwrites on next run). Bus-factor-1 acknowledged: the MCP server runs at acc.brokapps.ai; if down, agents lose `consult_scorecard` access but the markdown report + git index remain consultable. Identified at WP-SCP-023 023A plan-doc §9 D-043 reservation |\n| D-044 | 2026-05-09 | Adopt the WP-SCP-024 slice 024B-core operational contracts for cascade scaffolding. Components: (1) `scripts/scaffold-downstream.sh` (`SCP-073-scaffolder` per WP-SCP-020 §11 follow-up + WP-SCP-021 follow-up `SCP-075-scaffolder-compose`) generates adopter-side artefacts mechanically - bootstrap-only (refuses `CI=true` / `GITHUB_ACTIONS=true` per D-035 symmetry); validates `--scp-sha` against SCP main HEAD lineage (warns if not main HEAD; refuses if non-existent); `--scp-sha` 40-char hex regex; `--default-branch` enum {main, master, develop}; `--scorecard-emit` enum; `--output-dir` writability + path-normalised system-path denylist (incl `/private/var/*` macOS canonicalisation); emits `MANIFEST.json` with SHA256 audit; arguments per plan-doc §5.3. (2) `templates/adopter-wrapper.yml.tmpl` is the canonical adopter wrapper shape per ADOPT-001 §12 with Renovate inline-comment marker `# renovate: datasource=github-tags depName=jrnb2024/standards-control-plane-` enabling auto-bump per plan-doc invariant 8 Threshold A criterion; substitutions `{{DEFAULT_BRANCH}}`, `{{SCP_SHA}}`, `{{SCORECARD_EMIT}}`; least-privilege workflow-level permissions (`contents:read` + `statuses:write` only). (3) `scripts/check-invocation-log-entry.sh` CLI tool implements plan-doc invariant 2 cascade-status CI enforcement: 4 enforcement behaviours (fail-closed default for absent/unrecognised; `onboarded` -> log-entry-modified + target-match; `onboarded-operator-bump` -> log entry + `STATUS.md` `TF-024X-renovate-<slug>` row matching invariant 2 regex literally; `blocked-on-adopter-conflict` -> `DISPATCH-NOTE` `TF-024X-conflict-<adopter>` reference + log file NOT modified) + 5th carve-out for tooling slices (`cascade-status: not applicable` -> exit 0). Regex via `python3 re.search()` (PCRE-style); exactly-one-match assertion (closes multi-occurrence injection bypass); adopter-slug canonicalisation per plan-doc spec. CLI tool only - GitHub Actions workflow wiring (`.github/workflows/check-invocation-log-entry.yml`) defers to slice 024B-extras alongside `enable-required-check.sh --restore` mode + ADOPT-001 §12.8 break-glass procedure. D-044 was reserved for slice 024B at WP-SCP-024 plan-doc 024A merge (PR #102, 2026-05-04); after the operator-decided scope-split (SCOPE-CORRECTION-2026-05-09.md), D-044 stays scoped to core deliverables; sibling D-NNN may be reserved at 024B-extras authoring time for the deferred surface (`--restore` + break-glass + workflow wiring). Slice 024B-core ships under documented protocol deviation per feedback_four_tier_dispatch.md note + justify rule (kernel hook lives in ACC repo only; per-repo install pattern is FUP-ACC-INSTALL-TARGET-REPO-001 - open). | ACCEPTED | D-044 was reserved at WP-SCP-024 plan-doc 024A merge per the §9 reservation block. The original 024B accumulated 7 fix-rounds + 7 R-rounds against a single branch with each round closing 5-6 MAJ but introducing 2-3 new defects in just-added code (R6 missed primary-deliverable workflow wiring; R7 fix introduced bootstrap defect on slice's own PR). Empirical signal: surface area too large (16 files, ~3500 lines added) for single-pass convergence. Operator decided 2026-05-09 to split 024B into 024B-core (this slice; ~5 files; scaffolder + template + CI enforcement script + tests) + 024B-extras (deferred; `--restore` mode + ADOPT-001 §12.8 break-glass + `.github/workflows/` CI wiring + step 7 operator-interactive demo). Split is incremental delivery, NOT descope - both halves still ship; both required before 024C cohort onboarding opens. Original 024B work preserved at `feature/wp-scp-024-024b-extras-parking` branch (audit trail: 7 codex-fix-round-N-audit.log files + 7 R-round archive directories incl r8-archive carry-forward findings). Slice 024B-core scope is internally coherent: scaffolder generates adopter artefacts; CI enforcement script (CLI) validates cascade-status declarations; tests cover both. The CLI script can be invoked manually as a stop-gap until 024B-extras wires it into GitHub Actions. Bus-factor-1 acknowledged (D-031); 2026-07-21 quarterly review covers. Identified at WP-SCP-024 plan-doc §9 D-044 reservation |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
