026-05-01 | Grant `issues: write` permission to the `release-gate-violation-issue` job in `.github/workflows/release-gate.yml` (per WP-SCP-022 020H.3.1, closing TF-020H3rg-003). Privilege scope is the JOB level (not workflow default); the release-gate evaluation job continues to run with `contents: read` only. The new job fires only on `needs.release-gate.result == 'failure' && github.event_name == 'push'` — workflow_dispatch dry-runs and clean push:tags runs never reach it. The job's only mutation is `gh issue create` / `gh issue comment` / `gh label create --force` against the SCP source repo's own Issues namespace; no other write surface is unlocked. The auto-opened issue assigns `@jrnb2024` (single-operator mode per D-031); forward-compat env `SCP_RELEASE_GATE_ASSIGNEES` for 2nd-maintainer escalation. Symmetric with D-029 (020C.1 `statuses: write` for read-back commit-status) — a privilege-write expansion ratified by an amending decision row that names the precise threat-surface and bounded behaviour | ACCEPTED | The release-gate's last-line-of-defense alarm needs SOME write surface to surface beyond the workflow log; opening a GitHub issue is the lightest-weight signal that survives operator absence (the workflow log is ephemeral; the issue persists + assigns + sends a notification). Privilege-of-least-privilege is honoured by job-level scoping rather than workflow-level — the gate-evaluation steps (pip install, schema validate, ramp check, expired-config check) cannot accidentally open issues because they run in the release-gate job which has only `contents: read`. CODEOWNERS coverage on `.github/**` (per WP-SCP-020 020K) gates any change to release-gate.yml itself. The forward-compat env mirrors `SCP_REGRESSION_ASSIGNEES` from canary-replay.yml. Identified at WP-SCP-022 020H.3.1 R1 safety SAFE-MIN-003 + SAFE-MAJ-001 closures |\n| D-038 | 2026-05-02 | Adopt hash-pinned Python supply-chain installs across the SCP federation primitive's two YAML/JSON-validating workflows (`.github/workflows/policy-check.yml` × 4 install sites, `.github/workflows/release-gate.yml` × 1 install site) via `pip install --require-hashes -r requirements/policy-check.txt`. Top-level pins (`pyyaml==6.0.2` + `jsonschema==4.23.0`) match the prior inline pins exactly — no version bump on the public Python deps; only the install mechanism is hardened. Lockfile generated by `pip-compile --generate-hashes` from `requirements/policy-check.in`; full transitive closure (`attrs`, `jsonschema-specifications`, `referencing`, `rpds-py`) hash-pinned with all-platform wheel SHA256s. Closes WP-SCP-022 TF-020H3rg-002 (filed at 020H.3 R1 safety MIN-SAFE-006 — a PyPI compromise of either pinned version could substitute a malicious package on the next install; `--require-hashes` causes pip to refuse install when the served wheel's SHA256 is not in the lockfile). PATCH bump (v1.0.0 → v1.0.1) per `policies/VERSIONING.md` PATCH category (\"internal refactors of helpers/lib\"); no `policies/deprecations.yaml` entry required (no surface deprecation, no behaviour change for adopters). Lockfile regeneration command captured in `requirements/policy-check.txt` header AND `requirements/policy-check.in` documentation block | ACCEPTED | Symmetric supply-chain hardening with the pre-existing `scripts/scp-policy-check.lock` (OPA + Conftest + Regal binary SHA256 verification) and Sigstore attestation soft-warn for OPA (TF-007). All three layers — Python deps, OPA-family binaries, GitHub Actions SHA pins — are now hash-anchored at SCP source. The lockfile + `.in` source pair mirrors `scripts/.tool-versions` + `scripts/scp-policy-check.lock` (input + lockfile pattern). Bus-factor-1 acknowledged for lockfile regeneration: an operator who regenerates without re-reviewing the pip-compile output could introduce drifted transitive versions; mitigation is the `requirements/policy-check.in` source-of-truth (pins only the top-level deps; transitive resolution is automatic but reviewable in the diff). Future upgrades to `pyyaml` / `jsonschema` itself follow the same path: edit `.in`, regenerate `.txt`, review diff. The Sigstore re-tightening (TF-007) is parallel work on a different supply-chain dimension. Identified at 020H.3 R1 safety MIN-SAFE-006 closure |\n| D-039 | 2026-05-02 | Extend the slice 020M hash-pinned Python supply-chain install pattern to the SCP-self conflict-gate workflow (`.github/workflows/conflict-gate.yml` \"Install Python dependencies\" step) per WP-SCP-022 slice 020N, closing TF-020M-001. New `requirements/conflict-gate.in` (top-level pins matching `pyproject.toml` `[project.dependencies]` + `[project.optional-dependencies.dev]`: fastapi, uvicorn, httpx, jsonschema, pydantic, pyyaml, pytest, ruff, mypy) + generated `requirements/conflict-gate.txt` (pip-compile --generate-hashes under Python 3.12 to match `setup-python: python-version: \"3.12\"`; full transitive closure with all-platform wheel SHA256s). Install pattern: (i) `pip install --require-hashes -r requirements/conflict-gate.txt` (PyPI deps); (ii) `pip install --no-deps vendor/python/ct_auth-0.8.0-py3-none-any.whl` (local vendored wheel — not on PyPI; CODEOWNERS-protected via `vendor/** @jrnb2024`); (iii) `pip install --no-deps -e .` (editable local SCP package without re-resolving deps already hash-verified in step (i)); (iv) post-install version-pin assertion (`assert yaml.__version__ == \"6.0.3\"; assert jsonschema.__version__ == \"4.26.0\"; assert fastapi.__version__ == \"0.136.1\"; assert pydantic.VERSION == \"2.13.3\"`) defending against future-PR drift (mirrors 020M SAFE-MAJ-002 pattern). Deliberate pyyaml-version drift accepted: conflict-gate.txt resolves pyyaml==6.0.3, policy-check.txt pins pyyaml==6.0.2 — independent workflows with independent supply-chain anchors; both hash-anchored. **No semver bump.** Per `policies/VERSIONING.md` §\"Scope\", `conflict-gate.yml` is not in the public-surface enumeration (workflow_call inputs on `policy-check.yml`, schema contracts